<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noemvriana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1 December <a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/december-1">On this day: 1 December</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Noemvriana were the events of 1 December 1916 (18 November Old Style) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, when Greek royalist troops and militias clashed with British and French forces landed to enforce Allied demands on King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> withdrew after losses, and royalists then attacked supporters of Venizelos in the city. The Allies answered with a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and Constantine was forced to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in June 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/noemvriana.jpg" alt="Noemvriana" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Douglas MacPherson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noemvriana_birds-eye_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> stayed neutral when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, but its leadership was divided. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, while King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>, educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and married to Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, sister of Kaiser Wilhelm II, wanted a neutrality that suited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. In May 1916 the border fortress of Roupel was surrendered to German and Bulgarian forces, and the Allies suspected a secret understanding between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the Central Powers. After a military uprising of pro-Venizelos officers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, Venizelos, Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavlos-kountouriotis">Pavlos Kountouriotis</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panagiotis-danglis">Panagiotis Danglis</a> formed a provisional government there in October 1916, splitting the country in two.</p><p>The king secretly agreed on 23 October with the French deputy Paul Bénazet to hand over war materiel, but abandoned the agreement under pressure from his military advisers. On 24 November Vice Admiral Louis Dartige du Fournet gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> seven days to surrender at least ten mountain artillery batteries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A French and British force of 2,500 marines under Captain Henri Pugliesi-Conti, ordered to avoid violence, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> and found its positions around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> already held by Greek troops. After a rifle shot of unknown origin, fighting spread through the city, and the Allied squadron at Phaliron shelled areas near the stadium and the palace. The king agreed to give up six batteries, and by the early morning of 2 December the Allied troops had gone. Historian George Leon counts 194 Allied and 82 Greek dead and wounded, not counting civilians.</p><p>Royalist reservists, with arrests ordered under Generals Konstantinos Kallaris and Anastasios Papoulas, then ransacked the homes and shops of Venizelists: Leon gives 35 murdered and 980 expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Giannis Mourelos 14 civilians killed and 922 imprisoned across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, among them the mayor of Athens, Emmanuel Benakis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 2 December Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised Venizelos's government as the only lawful government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and on 25 December the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, under pressure from the palace, anathematised Venizelos. Du Fournet was relieved of his command, and in Britain H. H. Asquith gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a>, an admirer of Venizelos. Venizelos returned to Athens on 25 June 1917, and a united Greece joined the war; by autumn 1918 its 150,000 soldiers were the largest part of the Allied army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Macedonian front</a>, which earned Greece a seat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Noemvriana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greece was split in two, the National Schism, while Allied pressure on Athens led to the armed clash of December 1916, the <b>Noemvriana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-national-defence">Provisional Government of National Defence</a></li></ul></section>
